export const LESSONS = [
  // Module 1 — Digital Marketing and Google Ads Foundation
  lesson('dm-m1-l1', 'dm-m1', 1, 'Google Ads Accounts and Campaigns: The Foundation Structure',
    'A Google Ads account is the overall workspace for a client or business, and a campaign is a single structured advertising effort built inside that account with its own objective, budget, and targeting approach. In the source material, a campaign is never treated as a standalone Google Ads object: it sits inside a wider ecosystem that also includes Google Merchant Center (which supplies the product catalogue), Google Analytics 4 (which supplies audience and conversion data), and Google Tag Manager (which fires the tags that record conversions). Understanding this matters because campaign quality depends on the health of these connected systems, not just on settings inside Google Ads itself. A common mistake the sources warn against is treating campaign creation as an isolated task confined to the Google Ads interface, when in practice the account-level foundation — feed status, tracking setup, and linked reporting tools — has to be confirmed first.',
    5,
    'Performance Max Campaigns — Campaign Creation & Setup Standard (BGCT Ads Handbook), Applicable Platforms section'),
  lesson('dm-m1-l2', 'dm-m1', 2, 'Choosing Between Performance Max and Manual Shopping',
    "Performance Max and Manual (Standard) Shopping are both built on the same underlying product feed, but they hand different amounts of control to the advertiser. Performance Max gives Google's automated system control over targeting, placement, and creative combinations across all of Google's surfaces, using asset groups and audience signals to guide the algorithm. Manual Shopping is chosen instead when the team needs direct, granular control: setting individual bids per product group, deciding which products compete in which auctions, running campaign priority rules across multiple overlapping campaigns, keeping conversion tracking isolated to Purchase only for clean measurement, and guarding against the automated system overbidding on a low-margin or small product catalogue. The key idea to take away is that this is a deliberate trade-off between automation and control, matched to the catalogue and margin situation, rather than one format being a universal upgrade over the other. A mistake to avoid is assuming Performance Max is always the 'better' or newer choice — the sources describe specific catalogue and margin-protection reasons a team would choose manual control on purpose.",
    6,
    'Manual Google Shopping Campaign Creation (BGCT Ads Handbook), B.3 — Reason This Standard Was Selected section'),
  lesson('dm-m1-l3', 'dm-m1', 3, 'Conversion Tracking and the Merchant Center Feed as Pre-Launch Foundations',
    'Before any Performance Max or Shopping campaign is submitted, two foundations must already be in place. First, conversion tracking must be confirmed as firing correctly, and the conversion goal must be narrowed to Purchase only, with any secondary goals removed, so that the reported results reflect real completed sales rather than earlier-funnel actions. Second, the Google Merchant Center product feed must be in good health — approved products, no disapprovals — because the feed is described as the shared data foundation that both Performance Max and Shopping campaigns rely on to determine what can even be shown and how relevant it is judged to be. This is why feed optimisation is treated as an ongoing discipline rather than a one-off setup step: a feed problem does not just affect one campaign type, it affects both. A mistake the sources caution against is building or submitting a campaign before these two foundations are verified, since any performance data collected on top of unverified tracking or a weak feed cannot be trusted.',
    6,
    'Performance Max (P.Max) Feed Optimisation (BGCT Ads Handbook), B.2 — Advertising Objective section'),
  lesson('dm-m1-l4', 'dm-m1', 4, 'Governance Discipline: Paused-by-Default, Approval, and Evidence',
    'The sources describe a consistent governance pattern regardless of which campaign type is being built: every new campaign must be created and fully configured in a Paused status, and it may only be switched to Active after a designated approver has given explicit, documented sign-off. This separates the work of building and verifying a campaign from the authority to launch it, so a partially configured or unverified campaign can never begin spending by accident. Alongside this, the team is expected to capture and file evidence — such as screenshots or written records — at each verification step. The underlying principle is that a check which is not documented is treated as if it never happened, because documented evidence is what lets an approver or a later reviewer trust the work without needing a verbal assurance. A mistake to avoid is treating "Paused" as just a default resting state to click past quickly, or skipping evidence capture because a step feels too obvious to bother recording.',
    6,
    'Manual Google Shopping Campaign Creation (BGCT Ads Handbook), B.9 — Required Evidence section'),

  // Module 2 — Feed Optimisation and Merchant Center Readiness
  lesson('dm-m2-l1', 'dm-m2', 1, 'Why Feed Readiness Comes Before Campaign Creation',
    "In Performance Max, there are no keyword lists to manage directly the way there are in Search campaigns — the product feed itself is what Google uses to decide auction eligibility, bidding competitiveness, and which creative combinations get shown. That is why feed work is treated as the foundational, first-in-line activity rather than something to tidy up after a campaign is already live: a weak or error-filled feed has a direct, measurable negative effect on every campaign KPI, and errors compound over time as disapprovals build up. Before any feed optimisation work begins, a defined set of pre-requirements must be confirmed: an active Google Merchant Center (GMC) account linked to the Google Ads account, verified GMC login access, an identified product data source, access to a keyword research tool, documented client approval to edit titles and descriptions, access to the live website for price/availability checks, a reviewed Campaign Brief, and a feed backup taken before any bulk change. A mistake to avoid: if the GMC account has an active suspension, feed work must not proceed at all — the source treats this as a critical stop condition requiring immediate escalation to the Ads Team Lead, not something to work around.",
    6, 'Performance Max Feed Optimisation — BGCT Ads Handbook, Sections B.3 and G.1'),
  lesson('dm-m2-l2', 'dm-m2', 2, 'Core Feed Attributes: Titles, Descriptions, Categories, Images',
    "The source defines specific, non-negotiable formatting rules for the feed attributes that most directly affect whether Google can match a product to a search query. Titles should follow a [Brand] + [Product Type] + [Key Attributes] pattern (for example, colour, size, material) and must stay within 150 characters, with the first 70 characters treated as the visible portion in most placements — so the most important terms need to come first. Descriptions sit in a 500 to 5,000 character range, should open with the most important product information, and should expand on the title rather than repeat it verbatim. The product-category attribute must be set using Google's official numeric taxonomy ID, not a text label, because a blank or text-value category reduces auction eligibility. Main product images need a white or very light background with no watermarks, text overlays, or promotional graphics, must meet a minimum of 800 x 800 px (1,200 x 1,200 px is the recommended size so zoom functionality works), and every image URL must use HTTPS with no redirects; up to 10 additional images can be added for extra angles or context. A mistake to avoid: leaving the product-category attribute blank, or entering it as a text value instead of the numeric ID, is called out explicitly as reducing eligibility and wasting budget on the wrong auctions.",
    7, 'Performance Max Feed Optimisation — BGCT Ads Handbook, Section G.2, Phases 2–4'),
  lesson('dm-m2-l3', 'dm-m2', 3, 'Price, Availability, Identifiers, and Custom Labels',
    "Beyond text and images, the feed carries attributes that must stay in sync with the live site and with each other. Price has to match the live landing page price exactly, including the currency code, and availability (in stock, out of stock, or preorder) must reflect real-time stock rather than a stale snapshot — mismatches here are a direct cause of item disapprovals. Condition (new, used, or refurbished) must never be left blank. For product identifiers, a recognised product code (such as an EAN, UPC, or ISBN) should be added for every branded product, with a manufacturer part number used only when no such code exists; the brand attribute is mandatory for all products without exception, and the identifier-exists flag should only be set to false when a product genuinely has neither identifier — using it as a shortcut is flagged as degrading ad quality and eligibility. Custom labels (five per product) let products be grouped by margin, season, or performance tier to support bidding strategy, but the source is explicit that the label strategy must be documented and confirmed with the Ads Team Lead before it is applied, since labels cannot be edited retroactively once a campaign is live. A mistake to avoid: setting the identifier-exists flag to false across the board 'to save time' rather than checking each product individually.",
    7, 'Performance Max Feed Optimisation — BGCT Ads Handbook, Section G.2, Phases 5–7'),
  lesson('dm-m2-l4', 'dm-m2', 4, 'Quality Thresholds, Stop Conditions, and Review Cadence',
    "The source sets clear, numeric targets for what a healthy feed looks like: a feed disapproval rate kept below 1% of total product count, a product eligibility rate above 99%, and — as supporting campaign-level targets once optimisation is applied — ROAS of at least 4x, CTR of at least 1.5%, conversion rate of at least 3%, and impression share above 50%. The feed itself is also assessed against a 25-point internal scoring scale covering all the best-practice requirements (titles, descriptions, categories, images, pricing, identifiers, labels, shipping/tax, and error resolution); the source describes this 25-point scale but does not state the minimum passing score, so that number should never be treated as known or assumed. A firm stop condition sits above the 1% target: if the feed disapproval rate rises above 5%, work must stop immediately and be escalated to the Ads Team Lead before any further changes are made — this is treated separately from, and more urgent than, routine error clean-up. Ongoing review follows a set cadence: feed error review happens daily, title and description updates are reviewed weekly, a full feed audit happens monthly, and the automated feed refresh itself must run at minimum every 24 hours. A mistake to avoid: treating the 1% disapproval target and the 5% stop-and-escalate threshold as the same number — they trigger different actions.",
    6, 'Performance Max Feed Optimisation — BGCT Ads Handbook, Sections B.5, B.7, and G.6'),

  // Module 3 — Performance Max Campaign Creation
  lesson('dm-m3-l1', 'dm-m3', 1, 'Before You Build: Pre-Requisites, Strategy & the SKU Budget Rule',
    "Nothing gets built in Google Ads until every pre-requisite is confirmed done: the product feed is approved with zero disapproved products, Google Ads is linked to both Merchant Center and GA4, conversion tracking is already confirmed firing, the SKU count for the target collection is known, the daily budget has been approved in writing by the designated approver, creative assets have passed the brand visual review, the past-purchaser customer list is exported and ready, the GA4 14-day begin_checkout audience segment is built, and the Campaign Brief is complete. Any incomplete item is escalated immediately rather than worked around. With that in place, the workflow's first real decision is choosing a campaign strategy type — Product Category-Based, Price/Margin-Based, or Audience-Based — and documenting it, along with the business objective, target audience, and expected ROAS, in the Campaign Brief before anything is built. SKU count then decides the budget path: a collection with more than 100 SKUs may start at a daily budget of up to £25 once approved; a collection with fewer than 100 SKUs must wait for the designated approver to assign the opening budget rather than setting one independently; and a 'Death Listing' campaign is capped at a £10 daily budget as an absolute maximum, with the approver informed before the campaign is even created. A mistake the source flags directly: treating the under-100-SKU case as optional and setting your own budget anyway — the rule requires waiting for the assigned figure, every time.",
    10, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Campaign Creation & Setup Standard — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — Starting Point (Pre-Requisites) and Phase 1 Campaign Strategy & Pre-Build Planning'),
  lesson('dm-m3-l2', 'dm-m3', 2, 'Proving the Foundations: Conversion Tracking & Feed Validation',
    "Before a campaign can be created, two technical checks must pass — and both are gates, not formalities. First, conversion tracking: trigger a test purchase on the site and confirm the Google Ads conversion tag fires on the purchase thank-you page; separately confirm in GA4 that the purchase event appears with a correct value parameter; and confirm in Google Ads that the Purchase conversion action shows as recording conversions. If any one of these fails, escalate to the Technical Team immediately — the campaign is not created until tracking is confirmed working. Second, the product feed: confirm zero disapproved or pending products in the target collection, confirm product titles are front-loaded with high-intent keywords, and confirm custom labels are applied to segment products by margin tier. If any products are disapproved, the rule is to stop and resolve the disapprovals first — launching against a partially disapproved feed means those products cannot serve as Shopping ads at all, so the budget ends up wasted on Display or Video placements instead. This phase is also where the GA4 14-day begin_checkout audience segment (users who started checkout but did not purchase within 14 days) needs to already show 100 or more users; if it is below 100, the lookback window is extended to 30 days, and if it is still below 100 the segment is not attached — an In-Market audience is used as a substitute instead, and the situation is escalated to the Ads Team Lead.",
    9, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Campaign Creation & Setup Standard — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — Phase 2 Conversion Tracking & Technical Validation and Phase 3 Product Feed & Merchant Center Verification'),
  lesson('dm-m3-l3', 'dm-m3', 3, 'Building the Campaign and Its First Asset Group',
    "With every prior gate cleared, the campaign itself is built in a fixed order. Objective: Sales; campaign type: Performance Max. On the Merchant Center linking screen, confirm the correct account and feed are selected. Name the campaign using the client/collection/market naming convention. For bidding, select 'Conversion value' as the focus — never 'Conversions' — and enter the Target ROAS exactly as approved in the Campaign Brief. Enter the exact approved daily budget with no rounding. Set target locations and languages to match the brief, and record whether Final URL Expansion is on or off, with the reason, in the brief. Under Additional Settings there is a customer-acquisition safeguard that must be configured as part of this build — the workflow requires it to be set and never exceeded, though the specific value it should be set to sits outside this lesson. Conversion goals must be reduced to Purchase only, with any Add to Cart, Begin Checkout, or other secondary goal manually removed. The campaign is then built out with its first Asset Group: 5 to 20 images spanning all three required aspect ratios, each screened against the brand visual rules before upload; one video of at least 10 seconds; both a square and a rectangle logo; 15 short headlines (maximum 30 characters each); 5 long headlines (maximum 90 characters each); 4 descriptions (maximum 90 characters each) plus one long description (maximum 180 characters); and the business name entered with exact spelling and capitalisation. Before moving on, the Ad Strength indicator must read 'Good' or 'Excellent' — 'Poor' or 'Average' means adding more or stronger assets and re-checking, not submitting anyway. A mistake the source calls out directly: leaving Add to Cart or Begin Checkout active alongside Purchase — this lets the algorithm chase whichever goal is easiest to hit, usually a low-value micro-conversion, so reported ROAS looks healthy while real revenue stays flat.",
    10, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Campaign Creation & Setup Standard — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — Phase 4 Campaign Creation in Google Ads and Phase 5 Asset Group Setup'),
  lesson('dm-m3-l4', 'dm-m3', 4, 'Signals, the Quality-Score Gate, Approval & the Learning Window',
    "Two more phases finish the build before anything is submitted. Phase 6 adds audience signals and search themes to the asset group: up to 25 high-intent, transactional search themes, plus all three required signal types — a custom segment built from high-intent competitor/product search terms, the GA4 14-day begin_checkout remarketing segment, and the Customer Match list of past purchasers. Phase 7 applies exclusions and a final check: the shared negative keyword list, a brand exclusion if a dedicated Search campaign already targets the brand's own name, and a final read-through confirming ROAS, budget, goals, the acquisition safeguard, assets, and Ad Strength are all in order. Only then is the Campaign Quality Score calculated across five weighted dimensions out of a total of 25 — Campaign Strategy (5), Feed Creation & Data Quality (7), Campaign Settings & Bidding (5), Asset Group Quality (4), Audience Signals & Search Themes (3), and Monitoring & Iteration (1) — with a minimum passing score of 20 out of 25. Below 20, the campaign is not submitted; the lowest-scoring dimensions are fixed and the score is recalculated. The campaign is always built and kept in Paused status, then submitted to the designated approver — activation approval must be received in writing before the status is ever changed to Enabled; nobody self-approves. Once published, a reminder is set for the Day 28–42 post-launch window, which is the earliest point any structural change is permitted — making a structural change earlier resets the algorithm's learning and discards the data collected so far. Swapping an individual low-performing image or headline at the 14-day mark, after checking the Asset Performance report, is allowed and does not count as a structural change.",
    10, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Campaign Creation & Setup Standard — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — Phase 6 Audience Signals & Search Themes, Phase 7 Exclusions & Final Review, Phase 8 Post-Activation & Learning Window; Best Practice — Campaign Quality Scoring Framework'),

  // Module 4 — Asset Group Design
  lesson('dm-m4-l1', 'dm-m4', 1, 'Text Assets — Headlines & Descriptions',
    "A Performance Max asset group is the bundle of creative that Google's machine learning recombines to serve ads across Search, Display, YouTube, Discover, Gmail and Maps — the more valid, distinct assets it has to test, the more combinations it can try and the wider its placement coverage becomes. The asset group needs exactly 15 short headlines (each unique, each under 30 characters) and exactly 5 long headlines (each unique, each under 90 characters), plus 1 short description (under 60 characters) and 4 long descriptions (under 90 characters each). Because Google pairs headlines and descriptions together at random, every description has to make sense on its own rather than depending on a particular headline appearing next to it. A mistake to avoid: entering only a handful of headlines, or writing near-duplicate headlines that only reword the same idea — the handbook treats both as starving the algorithm of real combinations to test, which drags Ad Strength down.",
    7, 'Google Performance Max — Asset Group Setup — BGCT Ads Handbook, Success Criteria & Pitfalls — Common Advertising Mistakes'),
  lesson('dm-m4-l2', 'dm-m4', 2, 'Image Asset Requirements',
    'Three image ratios are required, each with a minimum of 3 images uploaded: Square 1:1 at a minimum of 1200x1200 pixels, Landscape 1.91:1 at a minimum of 1200x628 pixels, and Portrait 4:5 at a minimum of 960x1200 pixels. Two logo formats are also required: a 1:1 logo at a minimum of 128x128 pixels, and a 4:1 logo at a minimum of 512x128 pixels. Every image must be JPG or PNG and no larger than 5MB, and text overlay on any image must never cover more than 20% of the image area. A mistake to avoid: uploading an image where text covers more than 20% of it — the handbook is explicit that Google discounts or limits how often an over-texted image is shown, so it quietly loses reach even though it was technically accepted.',
    6, 'Google Performance Max — Asset Group Setup — BGCT Ads Handbook, Phase 6 — Upload Image Assets'),
  lesson('dm-m4-l3', 'dm-m4', 3, 'Video Asset Requirements',
    "Three video aspect ratios are required: a 16:9 landscape video (15–30 seconds), a 9:16 vertical video (15–60 seconds), and a 1:1 square video (15–30 seconds). Every video must be uploaded to the brand's own official YouTube channel and set to Public — a Private video simply cannot serve in a Google Ads campaign. Adding subtitles or captions to every video matters because, per the handbook, 85% of YouTube viewers watch without sound, so a video relying only on spoken audio loses most of its audience. Custom video also matters for a second reason: without it, Google auto-generates a low-quality slideshow from static images instead, which weakens brand perception and CTR. A mistake to avoid: leaving a video's YouTube visibility set to Private after upload — it will silently fail to serve rather than throw an obvious error.",
    6, 'Google Performance Max — Asset Group Setup — BGCT Ads Handbook, Phase 7 — Upload YouTube Videos & Link to Asset Group'),
  lesson('dm-m4-l4', 'dm-m4', 4, 'Extensions & the Launch Quality Gates',
    "The handbook details several extension types that expand ad real estate and give the algorithm more combinations to test: Sitelinks (minimum 2, target 2–20 active, sitelink text under 25 characters with two supporting description lines each under 35 characters), Callouts (4–10 active, each under 25 characters), Structured Snippets (at least 1 header with 3–10 values, each value under 25 characters), Prices (minimum 3 cards required before the extension will serve, header and description under 25 characters each), Promotions (1–2 active, item text under 20 characters, promo text under 15 characters), Calls (one validated phone number), Messages (message text under 35 characters), and Lead Forms for wholesale/B2B asset groups, which must show a visible privacy policy link and have their webhook tested before activation. Before any campaign is approved to launch, several quality gates must all be met: the Ad Strength indicator must read Good or Excellent, never Poor or Average; the Final URL must be tested and confirmed to load correctly with no 404 or redirect loop; UTM tracking parameters must be present on that URL; and conversion tracking must be confirmed active. A mistake to avoid: launching before conversion tracking is verified, or activating a lead form before its webhook has been tested — the handbook flags both as reasons to stop and escalate rather than launch.",
    7, 'Google Performance Max — Asset Group Setup — BGCT Ads Handbook, Phase 5 — Configure Sitelinks & All Extension Asset Types; Decision Points; Accident Spots'),

  // Module 5 — Audience Signals and Search Themes
  lesson('dm-m5-l1', 'dm-m5', 1, 'Signal Categories and Weighting',
    'Performance Max hands targeting decisions to an automated system, and the signals fed into an asset group are what steer it toward the right buyers. The handbook groups these signals into five categories and ranks them by strategic weight out of 25: First-Party Customer Data Injection (Customer Match uploads) carries the heaviest weight at 9, Strategic Search Theme Implementation is next at 8, High-Intent Interest & Behaviour Targeting sits at 4, and Demographic Refinement and Ongoing Signal Optimisation & Hygiene each carry 2. The weighting exists so effort is spent where it has the most impact — first-party purchase data and precise search themes do the heavy lifting, while demographic exclusions and routine hygiene work are lighter, supporting tasks rather than primary targeting levers. A documented mistake to avoid: uploading one unsegmented customer list instead of value-tiered segments, which trains the system toward average, unprofitable buyers rather than the highest-value ones.',
    6, 'Performance Max Audience Signal Strategy & Optimisation BGCT Ads Handbook — Signal Weighting Framework / Pitfalls — Common Advertising Mistakes'),
  lesson('dm-m5-l2', 'dm-m5', 2, 'Customer Match Requirements',
    "Customer Match lists are built from a client's own CRM or e-commerce data (email, phone, name) and uploaded through Audience Manager. Before a list can be used, the handbook sets two thresholds: the list must contain a minimum of 1,000 matched users after upload validation, and it must achieve a minimum 40% Match Rate. Both checks apply specifically to Customer Match CRM/CSV uploads. As a fictional illustration only: a list of 1,200 matched customers uploading at a 45% Match Rate would clear both thresholds, while a list of 1,300 matched users at only a 30% Match Rate would still fail and require re-cleaning before it can be attached. If a list falls below the 40% Match Rate, the required response is to re-clean the data, split it into smaller and more accurate segments, and re-upload — the list must not be attached to the campaign until it clears 40%. If re-cleaning does not resolve the shortfall within 48 hours, the situation is escalated to the Account Manager.",
    7, 'Performance Max Audience Signal Strategy & Optimisation BGCT Ads Handbook — Quality Standards / Decision Point 1 — Customer Match Rate Below 40%'),
  lesson('dm-m5-l3', 'dm-m5', 3, 'GA4 Audiences and a Common Metric Confusion',
    'GA4 custom audience segments are built for behaviours a customer list cannot capture, such as users who started checkout but did not purchase. Before a GA4 segment can be attached to a PMax asset group, it must show a minimum Audience Size of 100 active users. This is a different measurement from Customer Match entirely: GA4 audiences do not have a Match Rate, and the 40% Match Rate figure must never be applied to a GA4 segment — the handbook flags mixing these two metrics up as a documented, recurring mistake that produces incorrect quality checks. If a GA4 segment cannot reach 100 active users, the correct response is to extend the lookback window (for example from 14 days to 30 days) or broaden the triggering event; if it still cannot reach 100 users, it should not be attached, and an In-Market audience should be used in its place instead.',
    7, 'Performance Max Audience Signal Strategy & Optimisation BGCT Ads Handbook — Anti-Example 2 — Confusing Customer Match and GA4 Metrics / Decision Point 2 — GA4 Audience Size Below 100 Users'),
  lesson('dm-m5-l4', 'dm-m5', 4, 'Search Theme Caps and the Post-Launch Freeze',
    'Search Themes tell the system which search queries signal buying readiness, but Google Ads caps them at 25 per asset group — any themes submitted beyond that cap are rejected outright. The handbook allocates those 25 slots by category: 10 product-specific terms, 5 material/style attribute terms, 5 buyer-intent terms, and 5 competitor brand or model terms, totalling exactly 25. Themes must also be isolated per asset group — the same theme should never appear in two different asset groups, since overlap causes the system to compete against itself for the same users instead of optimising cleanly for either group. Once signals are saved on an asset group, a mandatory 10–14 day no-touch learning window follows: no bid, budget, or asset changes are permitted during that window, because any change resets the learning process and wastes the data already collected. A mandatory Insights review is then carried out at the 14-day mark to check search-category and audience performance before any optimisation changes are made.',
    7, 'Performance Max Audience Signal Strategy & Optimisation BGCT Ads Handbook — STEP 4 — Prepare Your 25 Search Themes / Quality Standards / Anti-Example 4 — Touching the Campaign During Learning'),

  // Module 6 — Bidding Strategy
  lesson('dm-m6-l1', 'dm-m6', 1, 'Do You Have Enough Data to Set a Target?',
    'Before you set any efficiency target (tROAS or tCPA) on a Performance Max campaign, you must check whether the account has enough conversion history for Google\'s bidding algorithm to work with. The source sets this threshold at a minimum of 15–30 conversions recorded on the primary conversion action (Purchase) within the last 30 days. In practice, 15 conversions in the last 30 days is the cutoff used to decide whether you may proceed: at or above 15, you may set a target in the next step; below 15, you must not. While checking, also confirm that only Purchase is configured as the primary conversion action — mixing in micro-conversions like page views or add-to-cart events would corrupt the count. If the account falls short of 15 conversions, launch the campaign on Maximize Conversion Value with no target set, then revisit after 30 days of data has accumulated; if it is still below 15 conversions at that point, the source requires escalating to the Ads Team Lead for a strategy review rather than guessing at a number. The mistake to avoid: setting an ambitious tROAS on day one with no conversion history — the algorithm has nothing to learn from, so it simply fails to find qualifying traffic and the campaign barely spends.',
    8, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Bidding Strategy & Optimisation (BGCT Ads Handbook), Quality Standards; Workflow — Step Sequence (Phase 1); Decision Point 1; Tutorial Step 1'),
  lesson('dm-m6-l2', 'dm-m6', 2, 'Choosing the Right Bidding Focus',
    "Performance Max asks you to tell the algorithm what to optimise for, and this choice matters more than almost anything else you configure. If the client sells products at noticeably different price points — say a fictional stationery retailer selling budget notebooks alongside premium desks — the bidding focus must be Conversion Value, so the algorithm chases high-revenue orders rather than treating every sale as equal. Conversions is only appropriate for fixed-value lead generation, where every lead is worth the same amount regardless of which one converts. The source is explicit that Maximize Conversions should never be used for a variable-price catalogue: because the algorithm is being told to maximise the count of conversions rather than their value, it gravitates toward the cheapest, easiest items to sell, driving up conversion volume while revenue stays flat. Somewhere around this stage of setup you will also encounter a New Customer Acquisition value, which lets the campaign apply an extra weighting for new-customer conversions; a New Customer Acquisition value must be configured for the campaign as part of this setup, applied at the campaign level rather than the account level. This source's guidance on the exact NCA value ceiling is inconsistent across its own sections, so this programme does not teach or test a specific number for it — see the Programme Sources screen for details. Document whichever bidding focus you selected, and why, in the Optimisation Log before moving on.",
    9, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Bidding Strategy & Optimisation (BGCT Ads Handbook), Workflow — Step Sequence (Phase 2); Accident Spots (Misaligned Bidding Focus); Tutorial Step 2'),
  lesson('dm-m6-l3', 'dm-m6', 3, 'Calculating a Conservative Starting Target',
    "Once the account clears the conversion-data threshold, you calculate a starting tROAS or tCPA rather than typing in the client's final goal directly. The source's rule is to set the starting target 15–20% less aggressive than the actual performance goal, giving the algorithm room to find converting traffic instead of choking on an unrealistic bar. In practice this is done by taking the account's actual 30-day average ROAS and multiplying it by 0.80 to land 20% below it. For example, if a fictional homeware account's actual 30-day ROAS is 250%, the starting tROAS would be roughly 200%, not the eventual 250% target. The same logic applies in reverse for tCPA: start higher than the real cost-per-acquisition goal, not lower. Alongside the target, check that the daily budget is at least 3–5 times the tCPA (or the revenue-equivalent for tROAS) so the campaign is not budget-constrained while it is still learning. The mistake to avoid: entering the client's actual target as the starting tROAS on day one — the source shows this as a common failure mode, since an efficiency bar set at the real goal is often too strict for an algorithm with no track record on the account yet, and spend stalls.",
    10, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Bidding Strategy & Optimisation (BGCT Ads Handbook), Workflow — Step Sequence (Phase 3); Quality Standards; Tutorial Step 3'),
  lesson('dm-m6-l4', 'dm-m6', 4, 'Protecting the Learning Phase and Scaling Safely',
    "After launch, the campaign enters a learning phase that the source defines as a 2-week (14-day) no-touch window. During these 14 days you must not change the bid strategy, tROAS/tCPA target, budget, creatives, or audience signals — any change resets the algorithm's exploration and forces it to restart, discarding the data collected so far. The only exception is a critical escalation approved by the Ads Team Lead. If ROAS looks poor in the first week, that is expected and not a reason to intervene. Once the 14 days pass, review performance against the approved target: if results are within 15% of target, leave the configuration alone. If the target has been met consistently for 2 or more consecutive weeks, you may increase the tROAS by 10–15% increments only, and budget by a maximum of 10–20% per increment — never doubling budget overnight. If performance is falling short instead, do not cut the budget; review search themes and audience signals first. The source also sets escalation windows: a campaign still spending zero budget 5+ days after launch must be escalated to the Ads Team Lead within 24 hours, and ROAS still below target 30 consecutive days after the learning window ends must also be escalated within 24 hours of that 30-day mark. If you hit a bidding situation this handbook simply does not cover, the source's No-Rule Procedure applies: stop, document what happened, escalate to the Ads Team Lead within 24 hours, and hold the campaign state unchanged until a resolution is given. The mistake to avoid: adjusting the tROAS every few days in response to short-term fluctuations — each change restarts the learning cycle, so the campaign never actually stabilises.",
    12, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Bidding Strategy & Optimisation (BGCT Ads Handbook), Workflow — Step Sequence (Phase 4–5); Decision Points 2–5; Escalation Points; No-Rule Procedure'),

  // Module 7 — Budget Management and Allocation
  lesson('dm-m7-l1', 'dm-m7', 1, 'Setting the Starting Budget by Campaign Category',
    'Before any daily budget is ever typed into Google Ads, the source requires identifying which of three categories a campaign falls into, because each one has a different rule. A Death Listing campaign (a narrow, low-priority listing type) is capped at a strict maximum of £10 per day — this ceiling is never exceeded, even briefly. A Standard Collection with 100 or more SKUs may start at up to £25 per day. A Standard Collection with fewer than 100 SKUs has no self-set starting figure at all — the source requires waiting for the designated approver to assign the amount in writing before anything is entered. In every category, the budget is only entered after written approval is received, and it must be entered exactly as approved, with no rounding and no independent adjustment in either direction. A mistake to avoid: assuming the under-100-SKU case is a minor exception that can be worked around by picking a reasonable number — the source treats waiting for the assigned figure as mandatory, not optional.',
    7, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Budget Management & Allocation Rules — BGCT Ads Handbook, Campaign Creation — Budget Allocation Rules table; Guidance — Phase 1 Campaign Creation Budget Assignment'),
  lesson('dm-m7-l2', 'dm-m7', 2, 'The Monthly Review and the Increase Trigger',
    "Every campaign's budget is reviewed on a fixed monthly date (the 29th of each month), using the 30-day ROAS figure as the primary signal. An increase is only ever applied when two conditions are both true at the same time: the 30-day ROAS is at or above 400%, and the campaign has also reached the minimum number of conversions in the last 30 days set for its current daily-budget tier (the source sets a sliding scale — smaller daily budgets need fewer conversions to qualify, larger ones need more). If either condition fails, no increase is applied, even if the other one looks strong. When both conditions are met, the permitted increase itself also follows a tier: smaller daily budgets can increase by a larger percentage (up to 20%), while larger daily budgets increase by a smaller percentage (as low as 5%) — every increase still requires approval before being applied. A mistake to avoid: increasing a budget because ROAS alone looks excellent, without separately confirming the conversion-count threshold for that specific budget tier has actually been met.",
    7, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Budget Management & Allocation Rules — BGCT Ads Handbook, Budget Increase Rules table; Decision Point 2 — Budget Increase Decision'),
  lesson('dm-m7-l3', 'dm-m7', 3, 'The Reduction and Shutdown Decision Path',
    'When a campaign does not qualify for an increase, the source requires working through a strict, ordered set of checks before ever cutting the budget — never reducing on 30-day ROAS alone. If the 30-day ROAS sits between 299% and 399%, the sequence is: check whether the 60-day ROAS is above 299% (if so, keep the budget unchanged); if not, check whether ROAS is trending upward recently (if so, keep it unchanged); only if both of those checks fail is a modest reduction applied, and only with approval. If the 30-day ROAS is below 299%, a similar but stricter sequence applies, ending with a check of the 90-day ROAS: if the 90-day figure is below 240%, this is the one condition serious enough to justify turning the campaign off entirely, and even that requires approval from more than one role before it happens. The reasoning behind all of this is that a 30-day window is often too short to judge a Performance Max campaign fairly, since short-term ROAS dips frequently self-correct as the algorithm continues learning. A mistake to avoid: cutting a budget the moment 30-day ROAS looks weak, without first checking the 60-day and 90-day windows the source requires — this is called out as the single most common cause of prematurely damaging a campaign that was already on the way to recovering.',
    8, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Budget Management & Allocation Rules — BGCT Ads Handbook, Budget Reduction & Hold Rules; Decision Points 3–4'),
  lesson('dm-m7-l4', 'dm-m7', 4, 'Approval, Evidence, and Protecting the Learning Phase',
    "Two rules sit above every specific budget decision in this source. First, no team member may independently set or adjust a campaign budget under any circumstance — every single change, including the very first budget entered at campaign creation, requires prior written approval, and every change must be logged with the reason, the ROAS data used, and the approval reference at the time the change is made, not afterwards. Second, any budget change made during the 10–14 day period immediately after a campaign launches or after a major change resets the Learning Phase, forcing Google's system to re-explore from scratch and typically making performance worse in the short term, not better. If the designated approver is genuinely unavailable and a decision is urgent, only the Ads Team Lead may grant a temporary approval — self-approval is never permitted, regardless of urgency. A mistake to avoid: increasing a struggling campaign's budget within its first 10-14 days in an attempt to fix low early performance — the source treats this as one of the accident spots most likely to make the problem worse, not better.",
    4, 'Performance Max Campaigns — Budget Management & Allocation Rules — BGCT Ads Handbook, Quality Standards; Pitfalls — Common Advertising Mistakes; Accident Spots'),

  // Module 8 — Performance Max Campaign Audit
  lesson('dm-m8-l1', 'dm-m8', 1, 'Why an Audit Assumes a Live Campaign, and Its Three Cadences',
    'This module picks up where campaign creation leaves off: the source is explicit that this audit and management process begins once a Performance Max campaign is already launched and active (or being taken over from a colleague, or reactivated) — not while it is still being built. Before any audit action is taken, a few basics must already be true: Editor-level access to the account, access to the linked Merchant Center account, conversion tracking already confirmed working, and the campaign\'s status confirmed as Active or Learning rather than Paused or Removed. The ongoing work itself runs on three cadences that never overlap in purpose: a short daily check (budget pacing and account/feed hygiene), a deeper weekly pass (search terms, placements, product-level performance, and creative quality), and a monthly strategic review (audience data refresh and budget reallocation across the account). A mistake to avoid: treating audit work as a one-off task rather than a recurring discipline — the source frames every one of these checks as something that must repeat on its stated cadence, not something done once and forgotten.',
    7, 'Performance Max (PMax) Campaign Management & Audit — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — G.1 Starting Point and G.2 Requirements Before Work Begins'),
  lesson('dm-m8-l2', 'dm-m8', 2, 'Daily Checks: Budget Pacing and Feed Health',
    "Every working day, two quick checks come first. For budget pacing, today's spend is compared against the target daily budget: a deviation of more than 10% is flagged for monitoring, and a deviation of more than 20% requires immediate investigation, since a spike or a stall this large can point to a tracking problem or a bidding error rather than genuine demand. Alongside spend, the campaign's ROAS is compared against its own rolling 7-day average — a drop of more than 25% against that average is treated as a same-day escalation trigger, not something to watch quietly. For feed health, the Merchant Center account linked to the campaign is checked for disapproved or suspended products: the target is zero critical disapprovals, and any issue found must be resolved (or escalated to the Technical Team) within 24 hours — left unresolved past 48 hours, disapproved products stop being eligible to show at all, quietly reducing delivery. A mistake to avoid: checking ROAS against yesterday's number alone instead of the rolling 7-day average — a single bad day is expected noise, but a sustained 25%+ drop against the week's average is the real signal the source wants escalated.",
    8, 'Performance Max (PMax) Campaign Management & Audit — BGCT Ads Handbook, Quality Standards — Budget Pacing, ROAS Monitoring, Feed Health; Guidance — G.3 Phase 1 Daily Actions'),
  lesson('dm-m8-l3', 'dm-m8', 3, 'Weekly Checks: Search Terms, Placements, Products, and Assets',
    "Once a week, four deeper reviews take place. Search terms are reviewed for high-cost, zero-conversion categories, which are added to the account-level negative keyword list (Performance Max only supports exclusions at the account level, never at the campaign level). Placements are reviewed for junk display or app traffic with high cost and no conversions, which are excluded via account-level placement exclusions. Product-level performance is reviewed to find 'zombie products' — items whose spend has exceeded a set share of that product's own price with zero conversions over a 14–30 day window — which are excluded or segmented out rather than left to keep draining budget. Asset quality is also reviewed: any asset rated 'Low' must be replaced within 7 days of that rating first appearing, and every asset group is checked against its expected slot counts, since an empty video slot causes Google to auto-generate a low-quality slideshow in its place. Alongside this, any bid-strategy adjustment made during the week must stay within a 10–15% maximum change, since a larger single adjustment risks resetting the campaign's learning. A mistake to avoid: adding a negative keyword or placement exclusion at the campaign level instead of the account level — Performance Max simply does not act on campaign-level exclusions the way other campaign types do.",
    9, 'Performance Max (PMax) Campaign Management & Audit — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — G.4 Phase 2 Weekly Actions; Accident Spots'),
  lesson('dm-m8-l4', 'dm-m8', 4, 'Evidence, Checklist Discipline, and Escalation',
    "Every action taken during an audit — daily, weekly, or monthly — must be entered in the Optimization Log with the date, the action taken, and the reason; the source states plainly that an undocumented change is treated as if it never happened. The compliance checklist behind this process is strictly binary: every item is answered YES/NO or DONE/NOT DONE, with no partial or vague answers accepted, and a single NO or NOT DONE means work stops until that item is resolved. Several situations have a defined escalation path with a stated response time: a ROAS drop of more than 25% goes to the designated approver the same business day; a Merchant Center disapproval left unresolved for 24 hours goes to the Technical Team within 2 hours; a conversion-tracking failure is escalated immediately, with all bid changes paused until tracking is confirmed working again; and any completely undocumented situation follows a stop-document-escalate procedure rather than being improvised. A mistake to avoid: making a bid or budget change while a conversion-tracking problem is still unresolved — the source requires pausing bidding-related changes entirely until tracking is confirmed fixed, since any decision made on broken data is unreliable.",
    6, 'Performance Max (PMax) Campaign Management & Audit — BGCT Ads Handbook, Checklist section; Guidance — G.9 Escalation Points'),

  // Module 9 — Shopping Campaign Creation: Automated Route
  lesson('dm-m9-l1', 'dm-m9', 1, 'What This Route Covers, and Pre-Launch Checks',
    "This module covers one of the two ways the source describes for creating a Standard Shopping campaign; a separate, distinct workflow (the manual route, covered next) exists for teams who need direct product-group-level bid control instead. The source itself does not spell out a specific definition of what makes this route 'automated' as opposed to the other — so rather than guessing, this module sticks to what the source does explicitly describe: a Target ROAS bidding setup with a defined learning phase. Before any campaign is built, three checks must pass: the target product collection's SKU count is confirmed in Merchant Center, the feed for that collection is confirmed free of active disapprovals, and the Purchase conversion action is confirmed as actively recording — if it is not, the source requires escalating to the Technical Team and not proceeding until it is fixed. A mistake to avoid: creating the campaign against a feed that still has unresolved disapprovals — the source requires resolving them first, since launching against a partially disapproved feed wastes budget on the products that are able to serve while the rest sit invisible.",
    6, 'Google Shopping Ads — Standard Campaign Creation, Setup and Management — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — G.3 Phase 1 Pre-Launch Compliance Check'),
  lesson('dm-m9-l2', 'dm-m9', 2, 'The SKU-Based Budget Rule and Naming Convention',
    'The starting daily budget depends entirely on how many SKUs are in the target collection: collections with more than 100 SKUs may start at up to £25 per day, while collections with fewer than 100 SKUs must have that budget scaled down proportionally rather than using the full figure. Every campaign is also named using a fixed seven-part formula — the ad type, the campaign owner, the Comparison Shopping Service used, the feed label, the product category or segment, the bidding-strategy abbreviation, and the target country — joined together in that order. One specific worked example of this naming formula that appears in the source is known to be defective: it uses the wrong type-of-ad label for a Shopping campaign, a leftover copy-paste error from an unrelated template, and it must never be used or taught as valid guidance. The formula itself, however, is sound: for example, a campaign for a fictional UK outdoor-furniture collection managed under this route might correctly be named "Shopping UK, team member, GCSS, GB, Outdoor Furniture, MCV, UK" — with the actual team member\'s name filled in. A mistake to avoid: copying a naming example that starts with the wrong ad-type label for a Shopping campaign — always build the name from the formula\'s field definitions, not from memory of an example.',
    8, 'Google Shopping Ads — Standard Campaign Creation, Setup and Management — BGCT Ads Handbook, Quality Standards — Budget Rule and Naming Convention; Guidance — G.3 Phase 2 Campaign Creation'),
  lesson('dm-m9-l3', 'dm-m9', 3, 'The Learning-Phase ROAS Setting and the Day-14 Review',
    "When Target ROAS bidding is first configured, the source is explicit that the initial value must be set low — 50% to 100% — rather than at the client's real target, for the first 14 days after launch. This deliberately gives the system room to gather conversion data before being asked to hit a strict efficiency bar. On Day 14, a review is mandatory (the source calls a missed Day-14 review a compliance breach in its own right): actual performance is checked, and if the data supports it, the Target ROAS is raised toward the confirmed post-learning target of 400%, with 300% treated as the floor it should not be set below once the learning phase has ended. If conversion volume at Day 14 is still too thin to set a confident target, the low-ROAS learning phase is extended for a further period rather than guessing at a number. Every campaign must also be built and kept in Paused status, and it may only be switched to Active once documented approval has been received from the designated approver — never before. A mistake to avoid: entering the client's real 400% ROAS target on day one instead of the required 50-100% starting value — the source shows this directly causes the campaign to under-deliver, since the algorithm has no conversion history to work from yet.",
    6, 'Google Shopping Ads — Standard Campaign Creation, Setup and Management — BGCT Ads Handbook, Quality Standards — ROAS Strategy; Guidance — G.3 Phase 4 Day 14 Review'),
  lesson('dm-m9-l4', 'dm-m9', 4, 'Common Mistakes and What Stays Excluded',
    "Beyond the rules above, a few recurring mistakes are called out directly: leaving the Merchant Center feed field blank during setup (which causes every feed in the account to be targeted instead of just the intended one), forgetting to remove secondary conversion goals like Add to Cart or Begin Checkout (which lets the algorithm chase an easy, low-value action instead of a real purchase), and forgetting to apply the tracking template before the campaign is reviewed (which breaks attribution once the campaign goes live). This programme deliberately does not teach a numeric value for the campaign's customer-acquisition bidding safeguard, and it never quizzes the defective naming example described earlier — both are excluded on purpose, and the reasons are recorded on the Programme Sources screen. A mistake to avoid: assuming any unresolved item can simply be fixed after activation — every one of the checks in this module must be verified while the campaign is still Paused, before it is ever switched on.",
    4, 'Google Shopping Ads — Standard Campaign Creation, Setup and Management — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — G.5 Common Pitfalls; G.6 Accident Spots'),

  // Module 10 — Shopping Campaign Creation: Manual Route
  lesson('dm-m10-l1', 'dm-m10', 1, 'Why Manual Shopping Is Chosen on Purpose',
    "The source is explicit that this route is selected specifically when granular, product-group-level bid control is needed — it lets the team set individual bids per product group, control which products compete in which auctions, apply campaign-priority rules across overlapping campaigns, keep conversion tracking isolated to Purchase only for clean measurement, and guard against an automated bidding system overbidding on a low-margin or small catalogue. This is a deliberate choice for a specific situation, not a fallback for teams who distrust automation in general. Before any setup begins, the same foundational checks apply as elsewhere: the Merchant Center feed must be active with the correct feed label for the target country, the Purchase conversion action must be confirmed recording, and the SKU count for the target collection must be known, since it determines the budget path. A mistake to avoid: choosing this route simply out of unfamiliarity with the automated one — the source frames it as a deliberate control-and-margin decision, tied to specific catalogue and bidding needs.",
    6, 'Manual Google Shopping Campaign Creation — BGCT Ads Handbook, B.3 — Reason This Standard Was Selected; Guidance — G.2 Requirements Before Work Begins'),
  lesson('dm-m10-l2', 'dm-m10', 2, 'Manual CPC Bidding and Product Group Segmentation',
    "Instead of a single automated efficiency target, this route uses Manual CPC bidding: a maximum cost-per-click is set manually, typically starting from the account's existing average CPC as a reference point, and Enhanced CPC is not enabled without approval. Immediately after the campaign is created, the single default product group must be subdivided — leaving a campaign running on the unsegmented, default group is treated as a setup mistake in its own right, because it means every product shares one flat bid with no way to prioritise higher-margin or better-converting items. Products are split by category, brand, custom label, or item ID, out-of-stock and zero-margin items are excluded from the active groups, and an individual Max CPC bid is assigned to each resulting group based on its margin and priority. A mistake to avoid: leaving the campaign live on the default, unsegmented product group — the source treats this as removing all of the bid-level control that is the entire reason this route was chosen in the first place.",
    7, 'Manual Google Shopping Campaign Creation — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — G.3 Phase 3 Bidding & Budget Configuration; Phase 5 Product Group Segmentation'),
  lesson('dm-m10-l3', 'dm-m10', 3, 'The SKU-Based Budget Scale and Naming Convention',
    'The same SKU-driven logic from budget management carries into Manual Shopping, but here it is expressed as a scale rather than a single cutoff: collections with more than 100 SKUs go to the designated approver for a budget decision (up to £25/day); the mid-range tiers scale down accordingly for smaller collections, and the smallest collections require asking the designated approver before proceeding at all rather than assuming a minimum figure. As with every other topic in this programme, the campaign\'s naming convention follows a fixed seven-part formula (ad type, campaign owner, Comparison Shopping Service, feed label, category, bidding-strategy abbreviation, country) — for example, a fictional 40-SKU outdoor-furniture collection targeting the UK might be named "Shopping, team member, GCSS, GB, Outdoor Furniture, ManCPC, UK", with an invented, proportionally-scaled budget consistent with the tiered rule. A mistake to avoid: reusing a budget figure seen on a different, larger campaign for a small collection just because it looked standard — the source treats this as a direct budget-waste risk, since a small catalogue cannot sustain the same spend as a 100+ SKU one.',
    7, 'Manual Google Shopping Campaign Creation — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — G.3 Phase 1 Pre-Setup, Step 4; B.8 — Naming Convention Standard'),
  lesson('dm-m10-l4', 'dm-m10', 4, 'The Fixed Build Order, Approval Gate, and Post-Launch Windows',
    "The source lays out eight phases — pre-setup checks, campaign creation, bidding and budget, other campaign settings, product-group segmentation, negative keywords, pre-launch review, and post-approval launch — and treats this order as fixed, not something to reorder for convenience. Just as elsewhere in this programme, every campaign is built and saved in Paused status, and it may only be switched to Active once it has received documented approval from the designated approver — that general approval-before-activation principle is taught here as a plain operational rule, without reference to any specific approval-record format or dispute over how that approval was documented. After launch, the campaign is monitored for the first 24 hours for budget pacing, conversion tracking, and any disapprovals, and a mandatory KPI review follows at the 14-day mark. A mistake to avoid: activating the campaign the moment setup feels complete, without first saving it in Paused status and waiting for approval — the source treats an unapproved activation as a serious compliance breach regardless of how confident the builder is in the setup.",
    4, 'Manual Google Shopping Campaign Creation — BGCT Ads Handbook, Guidance — G.3 Full Workflow: Planning to Post-Launch; G.9 KPI Tracking Points'),
];
